<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#northern-black-polished-ware">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northern Black Polished Ware</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 700–<a href="/years/200-bce">200 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Northern Black Polished Ware is a fine, glossy black or grey pottery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, made from about 700 to 200 BCE. Found mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>, it marks the period of the second urbanisation, when cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajagriha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kausambi">Kaushambi</a> grew and punch-marked coins came into use. Its high-quality finish suggests that it was a luxury ware.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/northern-black-polished-ware.jpg" alt="Northern Black Polished Ware" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_Polished_Black_Ware_Culture_(700-200_BCE).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The ware gives its name to an urban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> culture that succeeded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/painted-grey-ware-culture">Painted Grey Ware</a> and Black and red ware cultures. It developed in the late Vedic period and peaked from about 500 to 300 BCE, when the 16 great states, the Mahajanapadas, emerged in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, followed by the rise of the Mauryan Empire. According to Geoffrey Samuel, following Tim Hopkins, its centre in the Central Gangetic Plain was culturally distinct from the Painted Grey Ware culture of Kuru-Pancala to the west and developed towards cities and the use of iron on its own.</p><p>A transitional phase, proto-Northern Black Polished Ware, was first reported by Giovanni Verardi and his team at Gotihawa in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. It has a lustrous black surface with red spots left by problems in high-temperature firing, thick walls and the typical thali shape.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The period of the ware saw the first large cities of the subcontinent since the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, with massive embankments and fortifications, population growth, sharper social ranks, wide trade networks and crafts such as the carving of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, conch shell and semi-precious stone. Writing appeared in the Brahmi and Kharosthi scripts, including inscribed stamp seals, alongside a system of weights. Scholars note likenesses to the Harappan cultures, such as ivory dice and combs, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a> became more important. The culture may reflect the first state-level organisation in the subcontinent.</p><p>Around 200 BCE the ware gave way to red ware decorated with stamped and incised designs, but the same cities stayed inhabited and prosperous under the Shunga and Satavahana dynasties and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The ware has been found at sites linked to the Mahajanapadas from Charsada and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastinapura">Hastinapura</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>, Sravasti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a>, and east to Mahasthangarh and Wari-Bateshwar. Finds at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> show contact with the Gangetic valley. Shards from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> were once reported as the ware, but the archaeologist Phaedra Bouvet regards them as local KSK-Black Polished Wares, alike only in shape and style.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Northern Black Polished Ware” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The period saw what archaeologists call the second urbanisation of India: fortified towns such as Rajagriha, Champa and later Pataliputra grew along rivers and trade routes, the fine <b>Northern Black Polished Ware</b> spread as a luxury pottery, and silver punch-marked coins came into use.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its few stamp seals bear geometric designs but no inscription, unlike both the earlier Harappan seals and the later Brahmi-inscribed seals of the <b>Northern Black Polished Ware</b> culture, so its beliefs must be inferred from objects.</blockquote><a href="/culture/painted-grey-ware-culture">Painted Grey Ware culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
